/**

@page page_peano4_particles_mesh_traversal Particle interaction sets

\tableofcontents

Particle action sets build local and active particle sets while a spacetree
traversal emits its cell callbacks. These sets are temporary views over the
particle objects stored on adjacent vertices.

# Local and active sets

The local set contains the particles associated with the persistent vertices
of the current cell on its level. A particle can occur in the local sets of
several adjacent cells because the adjacent cells hold the same vertex.

The active set contains the local set and particles inherited from coarser
levels whose interaction region can reach the cell. It lets a fine particle
interact with larger particles stored higher in the tree. The action set builds
both views for the current callback; neither is persistent storage.

# Updating a particle once

A cell kernel filters its local particles with the cell marker's containment
test and a per-particle update marker. The action sets use
`CellHasUpdatedParticle` for this purpose. Without the marker, a particle on a
cell boundary can be updated by more than one adjacent cell.

A vertex callback sees each vertex set at one unique lifecycle point, but it
does not receive the active sets of neighbouring vertices. Particle-particle
interactions therefore normally run from cell callbacks.

# Interaction types

Multilevel interactions compare a local particle with the active coarse-level
particles. When the active particle is coarser, the same callback also applies
the reverse interaction: the coarse traversal cannot discover the fine
particle from its own active set. The implementation is
exahype2.particles.UpdateParticleMultiLevelInteractionSets.

Particle storage is described on @ref page_peano4_particles_realisation.

# Movement and sorting

The sets are valid only while every particle is associated with the closest
persistent vertex on the level selected for its search radius. Changing a
position or search radius can break that invariant. The sorting action sets
then lift the particle, move it, and drop it through refined levels over later
sweeps.

An application waits until the sorting and sieve state has settled before it
uses interaction sets that require the new associations. @ref
page_peano4_particles_mesh_consistency describes this lifecycle, rank
crossings, and tunnelling.

# Parallel execution

Particle containers reject subtree-task replication, so a particle-bearing step
traverses each rank serially. MPI exchange still maintains local and virtual
copies at rank boundaries.

*/
